            "text": "Gathered where forest and sea meet",
            "size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>At Dunrobin we aim to recreate some of the magic of the place: the formal gardens and woodland aromas, the elegance of the castle and a wider sense of landscape and heritage. The gin brings home in a bottle the feeling of standing in the garden after the rain, breathing it all in.</p><p>Its base is juniper, coriander seed and angelica root, which gives a balanced, classic feel with a forest-like openness. Lemon, rose-scented pelargonium leaf and blackcurrant leaf add lighter tones, and a light touch of clove brings a hint of warmth. The pelargonium is grown in tubs in the formal garden, which is open to the public, and by the castle entrance, and the blackcurrant is part of the kitchen gardens. The gin is made with spring water from the Big Burn.</p><p>We are proud to have worked with two experts in botanicals: Iain Crisp, Dunrobin’s head gardener, who has tended the garden and the surrounding woodland for 20 years, and Susanne Masters, an ethnobotanist who brought her expertise, creativity and guidance to the recipe work. Together they helped us include every element we felt was needed to represent such a place.</p>"
          }
